{"causes":["The Roman occupation of Judea created a socio-political context ripe for prophetic teachings.","The growing disenchantment with the existing religious authorities led to a demand for a new moral foundation.","Jewish traditions regarding blessings and covenantal relationships influenced the formation of the Beatitudes."],"effects":["The Beatitudes inspired the development of Christian ethical teachings and social justice movements.","They have influenced various interpretations of spirituality across diverse cultures and denominations.","The teachings have contributed to the establishment of fundamental Christian doctrines regarding humility and the value of the marginalized."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"jesus-christ","sourceName":"Jesus Christ","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"beatitudes","targetName":"Beatitudes","context":"Jesus Christ articulated the Beatitudes during the Sermon on the Mount around 30 CE."},{"sourceSlug":"sermon-on-the-mount","sourceName":"Sermon on the Mount","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"beatitudes","targetName":"Beatitudes","context":"The Beatitudes are a central part of the Sermon on the Mount, delivered in Galilee."},{"sourceSlug":"beatitudes","sourceName":"Beatitudes","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"civil-rights-movement","targetName":"Civil Rights Movement","context":"The Beatitudes inspired leaders of the Civil Rights Movement, including Martin Luther King Jr., in the 1960s."},{"sourceSlug":"beatitudes","sourceName":"Beatitudes","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"christian-teaching","targetName":"Christian Teaching","context":"The Beatitudes have been transmitted through Christian teaching and liturgy since the early Church."},{"sourceSlug":"sermon-on-the-plain","sourceName":"Sermon on the Plain","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"luke-gospel","targetName":"Gospel of Luke","context":"The Beatitudes also occur in the Sermon on the Plain found in the Gospel of Luke, highlighting different aspects of Jesus' teachings."}],"places":[{"name":"Galilee, Israel","role":"Teaching location"},{"name":"Jerusalem, Israel","role":"Cultural center"},{"name":"Rome, Italy","role":"Political authority"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Beatitudes","type":"Reference article","slug":"beatitudes"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q51643","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatitudes"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Beatitudes contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Beatitudes (from Latin: beati, 'blessed' or 'happy') are the opening declarations of the Sermon on the Mount — the extended teaching attributed to Jesus of Nazareth in the Gospel of Matthew (chapters 5–7) and its shorter parallel in Luke 6:20–23 (the Sermon on the Plain). The Matthean Beatitudes (Matthew 5:3–12) comprise eight or nine blessings — 'Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven; Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted; Blessed are the meek, for they will inherit the earth...' — that articulate a radical inversion of conventional worldly values: the poor, the meek, the persecuted, those who hunger and thirst for righteousness are declared the recipients of divine blessing and the inheritors of the Kingdom of God. The Beatitudes form the programmatic opening of Jesus's most comprehensive surviving discourse, and the Sermon on the Mount as a whole is considered by many Christians the definitive statement of Christian ethics.

The Beatitudes' social and ethical radicalism — their blessing of the poor, the mournful, and the persecuted rather than the powerful, the prosperous, and the celebrated — has made them one of the most contested and transformative passages in Western history. Medieval Catholic interpretation generally spiritualised them (the 'poor in spirit' as the spiritually humble rather than the materially poor), while Francis of Assisi's movement found in them a mandate for voluntary poverty, and the liberation theology movement of the 20th century returned to a more literal reading of Jesus's blessing of the poor as a critique of economic inequality. Tolstoy, Gandhi, and Martin Luther King each drew on the Beatitudes' non-violent ethic ('Blessed are the peacemakers') in their programmes of civil resistance.

The Sermon on the Mount, of which the Beatitudes are the opening, also contains the Lord's Prayer, the Golden Rule ('Do to others as you would have them do to you', Matthew 7:12), and the antitheses ('You have heard it said... but I say to you') that intensify the Torah's moral demands. Together they constitute the clearest surviving statement of Jesus's ethical teaching and have shaped Christian moral theology, social ethics, and political thought across two millennia.","subjects":["New Testament","Christian Ethics","Classical Era","Jesus of Nazareth","Sermon on the Mount","Christianity","Non-Violence","Liberation Theology"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Beatitudes significantly shaped Christian ethics, influencing moral teachings and social justice movements worldwide, with direct impacts on events like the Civil Rights Movement.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
